{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond II of Marseilles was born in 1140 and died in 1187. He was a count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. He succeeded his father as count of Tripoli when he was twelve years old.\n\nRaymond III was married twice. His first wife was Eschiva of Bures. She was the daughter of Elinard prince of Bures and Cecilia of Farroukh. Raymond III and Eschiva of Bures had two children together. Their son was Bohemond III of Antioch and their daughter was Lucia of Tripoli. After Eschiva's death, Raymond III married his second wife, Maria Comnena. She was the daughter of John Comnenus the nephew of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Comnenus. Raymond III and Maria Comnena had one son together, named Bohemond IV of Antioch.\n\nDuring Raymond III's reign as count of Tripoli, he faced many challenges. He had to deal with the threat of the Zengids, a Muslim dynasty that controlled much of the Levant. In 1157, he allied himself with the Byzantine Empire and participated in a campaign against the Zengids. However, the campaign was unsuccessful, and Raymond III was forced to retreat to Antioch.\n\nIn 1164, Raymond III was captured by the Zengids during the Battle of Harim. He was held captive for seven years before being released in 1171. During his captivity, his wife, Eschiva of Bures, served as regent of Tripoli.\n\nAfter his release, Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued to face challenges from the Zengids and other Muslim rulers. He also had to deal with internal conflicts within the Crusader states. In 1183, he was involved in a dispute with Bohemond III of Antioch, his son from his first marriage. The dispute was resolved with the intervention of the King of Jerusalem, Baldwin IV.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin. The battle was a major defeat for the Crusader states, and it marked the beginning of the end of the Crusader presence in the Levant. Raymond III was succeeded as count of Tripoli by his son, Bohemond III of Antioch.\n\n*Highlighted section part 1: Early life*\n\n*Highlighted section part 2: Marriages and children*\n\n*Highlighted section part 3: Reign as count of Tripoli*", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.3794739544391632}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -8.940656698541716e-06}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "also", "logprob": -0.515670657157898}, {"token": "known", "logprob": -0.0033385988790541887}, {"token": "as", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -1.549708758830093e-05}, {"token": "II", "logprob": -0.5765655040740967}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -0.00020776021119672805}, {"token": "Mar", "logprob": -1.7202842235565186}, {"token": "se", "logprob": -0.0059780846349895}, {"token": "illes", "logprob": -1.0537045001983643}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.045628007501363754}, {"token": "born", "logprob": -0.900700569152832}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.10332086682319641}, {"token": "", "logprob": -0.0006641327636316419}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.001032891683280468}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": "and", "logprob": -0.022165650501847267}, {"token": "died", "logprob": -0.0005818104837089777}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -0.00012337400403339416}, {"token": "", "logprob": -0.0002935693773906678}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": "7", "logprob": -7.152555099310121e-07}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.3885166347